## What happened

Apple removed DSA support from its native cryptography libraries (Security.framework / CommonCrypto). On macOS, .NET's `DSA.Create()` delegates to the OS crypto stack, so once Apple dropped it, .NET 11 began throwing **`PlatformNotSupportedException`** on macOS. Windows and Linux are unaffected because their crypto backends (CNG and OpenSSL) still support DSA.

## How to fix it

You have two options, depending on whether you can change the signature algorithm:

### Option 1 – Migrate to a modern algorithm (recommended)
Replace DSA with **ECDSA** or **Ed25519**. DSA is considered legacy; ECDSA is the direct successor and is supported on all three platforms:

```csharp
using var ecdsa = ECDsa.Create(ECCurve.NamedCurves.nistP256);
byte[] signature = ecdsa.SignData(data, HashAlgorithmName.SHA256);
```

This requires re-signing existing data with the new key.

### Option 2 – Use OpenSSL on macOS (if you must verify existing DSA signatures)
Load OpenSSL explicitly so you bypass Apple's stack:

1. Install OpenSSL on the Mac (`brew install openssl`).
2. Use the `DSAOpenSsl` interop class (available since .NET 7):

```csharp
using System.Security.Cryptography;
using System.Runtime.InteropServices;

DSA dsa;
if (RuntimeInformation.IsOSPlatform(OSPlatform.OSX))
{
    // Use OpenSSL backend directly
    dsa = new DSAOpenSsl();          // requires OpenSSL to be installed
}
else
{
    dsa = DSA.Create();
}

dsa.ImportSubjectPublicKeyInfo(publicKeyBytes, out _);
bool valid = dsa.VerifyData(data, signature, HashAlgorithmName.SHA1);
```

### Summary

| Approach | Pros | Cons |
|---|---|---|
| Migrate to ECDSA/Ed25519 | Future-proof, no extra deps | Must re-sign existing data |
| `DSAOpenSsl` on macOS | Keeps verifying legacy sigs | Requires OpenSSL install on macOS |

For a verification-only library, **Option 2** is the lowest-friction path—it lets you keep validating existing signatures without re-issuing them. Long-term, plan to migrate to ECDSA.
